Hmm, but existing two-part range behavior has its own quirky semantics (end<0 → max(max+end, 0), i.e. len+end; start<0 → 0). Existing non-stepped semantics must be unchanged. So for stepped slices I'll implement Python-ish semantics as above; for two-part keep existing code path.

Wait, but the requirement says stepped range must work in both directions and "Use the same index-selection semantics as read slicing" for assignment. So I need a single function computing selected indexes for a stepped slice, used by both read and assignment.

Hmm, but what about existing two-part range read slicing — does it stay byte-based for strings? Requirement 4: "String indexing and range slicing must operate on Unicode characters (runes), not raw bytes. This includes single index access and both two-part and three-part ranges." So we must convert existing string indexing to rune-based! Existing code uses `stringObject.Value[idx]` on bytes. For "123" same thing. But for unicode strings it changes behavior. Also existing error `index ranges can only be numerical` uses end.Inspect etc.

Careful: existing tests expect `"123"[1]` == "2" etc. Rune-based works.

Now the error ordering requirement: "A non-numeric `start` in array/string slices must keep the existing index-operator error format". Currently the dispatch produces this error when index isn't NUMBER. E.g. `[1,2,3]["a"]` → "index operator not supported: a on ARRAY". For a range `[1,2,3]["a":2]`? Currently: index is STRING → default → "index operator not supported: a on ARRAY". Keep.

But for stepped slice with omitted start (Index nil → NULL), we must not error. So dispatch logic:

```go
